syntax:
To overload the function call operator, define a member function in a class like this:

return_type operator()(parameter_list) const;  // Example: int operator()(int val) const;

Practical usage notes: The 'const' qualifier is recommended if the operation doesn't modify the object, allowing it to work with const contexts. Parameters can vary based on needs, and this operator can access class members, enabling stateful operations. In real-world code, combine this with lambdas or std::bind for more idiomatic C++.

code_example:
#include <iostream>

class Abs {
public:
    int operator()(int val) const {  // Overloaded call operator
        return val < 0 ? -val : val;  // Returns the absolute value
    }
};

int main() {
    Abs absObj;  // Create an object of the class
    int i = -5;
    int ui = absObj(i);  // 'Call' the object like a function
    std::cout << "Absolute value: " << ui << std::endl;  // Outputs: Absolute value: 5
    return 0;
}
